00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:03.350
Jack: So, what is meant when we talk
about Imperative and Declarative coding?

00:00:04.460 --> 00:00:08.280
Leah: Imperative coding or programming
can directly change the ‘State’

00:00:08.309 --> 00:00:11.919
of the program through statements
which provide instructions, or

00:00:11.950 --> 00:00:15.090
anything in the memory that holds
the current values of your program.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:19.740
Imperative statements are grouped into
sections which are called procedures.

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:24.310
When called, a procedure will run
the input through a set of statements

00:00:24.350 --> 00:00:28.269
and return an output or change the
program to the desired ‘state’.

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:31.900
Jack: For example, let’s say we
have a data structure containing

00:00:31.929 --> 00:00:35.000
individual student grades,
and which class they are in.

00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:38.940
We then want to extract
all grades from Class C.

00:00:39.970 --> 00:00:44.240
An Imperative procedure might express
[in code] the following statements:

00:00:44.809 --> 00:00:45.149
1.

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:50.089
Create a new variable called ‘Class
C results’ to collect results

00:00:51.139 --> 00:00:51.149
2.

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:54.870
Go through one student at a
time, and check the following:

00:00:55.650 --> 00:00:56.129
3.

00:00:56.930 --> 00:00:58.570
Is the student in Class C

00:00:59.400 --> 00:00:59.860
4.

00:01:00.340 --> 00:01:03.670
If they are, add their
grades to ‘Class C results’

00:01:04.309 --> 00:01:04.839
5.

00:01:05.570 --> 00:01:09.330
If they are not, move to the next
student and continue this procedure

00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:10.940
until all have been checked

00:01:11.750 --> 00:01:15.380
Leah: Because imperative source
code can be low-level, we use

00:01:15.380 --> 00:01:19.229
procedures to make a step towards
higher-level declarative programming.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:22.549
Let’s look at an example of this in code.

00:01:29.049 --> 00:01:32.030
Now let’s look at Declarative
coding or programming.

00:01:33.210 --> 00:01:36.810
Most forms of Declarative
programming do not support change

00:01:36.810 --> 00:01:38.370
to the ‘State’ of a program.

00:01:39.030 --> 00:01:42.649
The programs are run based on a
series of ‘expressions’ which specify

00:01:42.670 --> 00:01:46.809
exactly what the program should
accomplish, rather than how it does it.

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:50.190
This is accomplished through
logical statements, or those

00:01:50.190 --> 00:01:51.520
that describe the function.

00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:56.130
Let’s go back to our example which
contains student grades and classes.

00:01:56.760 --> 00:02:02.170
We want to achieve the same result as
before, ‘extract all grades from Class C’.

00:02:02.980 --> 00:02:07.130
Our declarative statement may be more
direct, for example saying [in code]:

00:02:07.940 --> 00:02:12.200
Jack: EXTRACT all grades from
students in Class C and ADD them

00:02:12.550 --> 00:02:16.850
to a new variable ‘Class_C_results’

00:02:17.719 --> 00:02:22.579
The output is the same as with imperative
programming, but the result was achieved

00:02:22.579 --> 00:02:27.280
with an expression of what the results
should be, not how to get them, as

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:29.040
this is implicit in the program.

00:02:29.790 --> 00:02:31.850
Now let’s look at an
example of this in code.

